      "fact": 1,
      "headline": "The Umber Host Marches",
      "text": "The air at Winterfell tasted of dust and dried pine. Jon Umber sat atop his great destrier, the weight of his mail pulling at his shoulders as he watched the columns begin to snake southward. Behind him, the massive levy of three thousand strong moved like a slow, iron river, followed by the glovers, the norreys, and the liddles. A smaller, more silent contingent of Boltons moved alongside them, their armor dark and unreflective. Lord Umber caught the gaze of Roose Bolton; the man was a pale shadow in the sunlight, his eyes cold and his lips pressed into a thin, bloodless line. He said nothing, merely adjusted his furs as the drums began to beat, signaling the march toward Moat Cailin.",
      "pov": "Jon Umber"
    },
    {
      "fact": 2,
      "headline": "A Departure for the Wall",
      "text": "The night was sharp and biting, a reminder that summer was a fleeting dream. Jon felt the heavy weight of Benjen’s letter tucked deep within his saddlebag, a secret cargo that felt heavier than any steel. He tightened the cinch on his horse, his breath blooming in white plumes before his face. Six men of his household moved through the shadows, their faces grim in the moonlight. A flash of white fur darted through the gloom—Ghost, running ahead into the darkness of the Wolfswood, a silent ghost indeed. With a pull on the reins, Jon turned his back on the warmth of the keep and faced the long road to Castle Black.",
      "pov": "Jon Snow"
    },
    {
      "fact": 3,
      "headline": "Silence at the Moat",
      "text": "The stones of the causeway lay cleared, the wreckage of old wars swept aside to make a path. Robb Stark stood amidst the ruin of the three great towers, his eyes scanning the horizon where the road met the sky. The archers stood atop the battlements, their bows gripped tight, their eyes searching the treeline for movement. The wind whistled through the gaps in the masonry, carrying nothing but the scent of damp earth and old stone. He had his men, and he had the towers, but the silence from the south was a heavy, suffocating thing.",
      "pov": "Robb Stark"
    }
  ]
}

